
== Set Up New Replication

First-time setup is done through the Configure Canvas. The Canvas makes it possible to quickly get connected to one or more endpoints and configure tables, subset rules, and transforms before SymmetricDS is installed in any database. This is all done through a graphical interface that makes it easy to visualize the configuration.

If you have a CSV or SQL file containing the configuration from another installation of SymmetricDS, it can be imported into the Configure Canvas when setting up a new installation of SymmetricDS. The Canvas can be used to modify the imported configuration if necessary.

=== Connect to the First Endpoint

image::images/canvas/DefineEndpointScreen_first.png[]

The first step in setting up new replication is to connect to one of the endpoints that will be responsible for synchronizing.

* Specify the endpoint type.
* Specify a display name for the endpoint.
* Specify the endpoint connection information. Select the Basic tab to specify the host, port, and database name separately. Alternatively, select the Custom tab to specify the full JDBC URL, where an example will be provided based on the selected database type.
* Specify the user ID to be used to log in to the endpoint.
* Specify the password to be used to log in to the endpoint.

As the endpoint type and display name change, the changes will be reflected in the Configure Canvas on the left. When proceeding to the next screen, SymmetricDS will verify that it can connect to the endpoint using the provided information.

=== Connect to Additional Endpoints

image::images/canvas/DefineEndpointScreen_second.png[]

The second step in setting up new replication is to connect to a second endpoint that the first endpoint will synchronize with. Use the Endpoint field to choose between existing endpoints or a new endpoint. The initial setup process starts off with just two endpoints, but the Canvas can be used to configure any number of endpoints for synchronization.

=== Table Configuration

image::images/canvas/SelectTablesScreen.png[]

Specify which tables to synchronize using the Select Tables screen. If both endpoint types can be used as a source, then there will be two links to configure: one from the first endpoint to the second endpoint and another from the second endpoint to the first endpoint. Start adding tables by clicking one of the Add buttons.

image::images/canvas/AddTablesDialog.png[]

Select all of the tables that should be synchronized in the given direction. Use the catalog and schema fields to choose the source and target catalog and schema, and use the filters to narrow down the available and selected tables separately. Clicking the Refresh tables button will re-read the tables from the endpoint. Advanced options include a prefix that SymmetricDS will append to its trigger IDs, an option to use a non-default channel, and an option to include foreign key parent tables.

image::images/canvas/AddTablesDialog_bidirectional.png[]

After table selection, there is an option to configure the selected tables bidirectionally.

image::images/canvas/ViewTablesDialog.png[]

image::images/canvas/RemoveTablesDialog.png[]

Once a group of tables has been configured for synchronization, the Canvas will update and display how many tables are configured to synchronize in each direction. Clicking the X icon to the left of the group of tables will remove these tables from synchronization. Also, there are options to view all selected tables or remove individual tables from being synchronized across a link.

=== Subset Rule Configuration

[.float-group]
--
[.left.text-left]
image::images/canvas/SubsetDataScreen.png[]

Specify subsets of data that get synchronized to different target endpoints using the Subset Data screen. Click the New Subset Rule button to get started.

--

image::images/canvas/SubsetRuleDialog_0.png[]

Specify a name for the new subset rule and the link that it will apply to. Determine whether the subset rule should use data from the same table or from a separate related table.

If the rule should use data from the same table, specify the name of the column that it should check and what kind of ID this column's value represents. The EXTERNAL_ID has meaning to the user and provides integration into the system where it is deployed. For example, the EXTERNAL_ID might be a retail store number or a region number. It is possible to assign the same EXTERNAL_ID to multiple nodes. Unlike the EXTERNAL_ID, the NODE_ID is a unique identifier for each node. The NODE_ID is generated by SymmetricDS when a node registers. The NODE_GROUP_ID identifies the node group that the node belongs to. Synchronization is specified between node groups, which means you only need to specify it once for multiple nodes in the same group.

If the rule should use data from a separate related table, specify the name of the related table's column that it should join to.

image::images/canvas/SubsetRuleDialog_1.png[]

Select the tables that the new subset rule should apply to. The column name that was previously specified is used to narrow down which tables can be selected.

image::images/canvas/SubsetRuleDialog_2.png[]

If the rule should use data from a separate related table, then there is the option to join to one external table or multiple external tables to determine the target endpoints. Joining to one external table requires the table's name as well as the column name to check for an external ID. Joining to multiple tables requires a select statement that returns a result set of the target node IDs that data should be sent to. In this case, the beginning of the select statement is already written and only the where clause needs completed. Row data can be substituted into the where clause using uppercase column names preceded with a colon character (e.g., ":MY_COLUMN").

Once a subset rule has been configured, it is possible to remove it by clicking the X icon on the left or to expand it to view its details. Once expanded, clicking the pencil and paper icon next to the rule name allows the rule to be renamed.

=== Transform Configuration

[.float-group]
--
[.left.text-left]
image::images/canvas/TransformDataScreen.png[]

Configure transforms that will be applied to data as it gets extracted from the source endpoint or loaded into the target endpoint using the Transform Data screen. Click the Add transform button to start creating an extract transform, or start creating a load transform using the arrow on the right.

--

image::images/canvas/TransformDialog.png[]

If a source table and a target table share the same name and the source table is configured to sync to the selected target catalog and schema, then there will be a dashed line to indicate how data will be synchronized by default. Transforming the source table will override this auto mapping.

Create a transform by dragging a line from a source table to a target table, or by clicking a source table and then clicking a target table. The catalog and schema fields determine which tables are displayed on either side and the filter fields can be used to narrow down the tables on each side separately. Additionally, tables with or without transform mappings can be hidden using the radio buttons on the right.

Once a transform has been created, a solid line will appear connecting the source and target tables. A transform or an auto mapping can be selected by clicking the source table, the target table, or the line connecting the two tables. Clicking the source or target table will also select all other transforms and auto mappings that connect to that table. To deselect, click the background or select a different transform or auto mapping.

Once a transform or auto mapping has been selected, a few options appear on the right. The Delete button will delete all selected transforms, but it will not delete auto mappings. The Edit button opens a dialog that allows the table-level details of the transform to be edited. The Map Columns button opens a different dialog that allows the columns to be mapped from source to target. If an auto mapping is modified, it will be replaced by a transform.

[.float-group]
--
[.left.text-left]
image::images/canvas/TransformDetailsDialog.png[]

There are several settings that can be changed when editing the table-level details of a transform. These settings are described in the <<Transforms>> section.

--

image::images/canvas/TransformColumnsDialog.png[]

Column-level transforms can be created, selected, and filtered in a similar way to table-level transforms. By default, if a source column and a target column share the same name, the source column's value will be copied over to the target column, indicated by a dashed line. This is the behavior of a table-level transform that has an implied column policy. To override this behavior for one column pairing, create a transform that connects to the source column and/or the target column. To override this behavior for the entire table-level transform, click the Switch to Specified button on the right to toggle the transform's column policy to specified. It can be toggled back to implied by clicking the Switch to Implied button.

Below the list of source columns, there is the option to create a transform with no source column. On the opposite side, below the list of target columns, there is the option to create a transform that removes the mapping for a source column by getting rid of its value. This option is only necessary if the table-level transform's column policy is implied.

Once a column transform has been selected, it will be possible to delete or edit the column transform using the icons on the transform or the buttons on the right. The Edit button opens a dialog that contains options regarding how the individual column is transformed.

image::images/canvas/TransformColumnDetailsDialog.png[]

Here it is possible to configure individual column transforms for inserts, updates, and deletes, or a single column transform to apply to all three. If a column transform is enabled, it will have a 1 in its tab. If a column transform is disabled, it will have a 0 in its tab and when the tab is selected, the <disabled> option will be selected on the left side. The primary key and column transform order options are described in the <<Columns,Transform Columns>> section, while each column transform type is described in the <<Transform Types>> section.

image::images/canvas/TransformColumnsDialog_2.png[]

Once a column transform has been defined, a solid line will appear with each transform type listed. If there are no transform types listed, this indicates that the value will be copied from source to target. In addition, the icons next to the column names will indicate whether the column has been marked as a primary key.

[.float-group]
--
[.left.text-left]
image::images/canvas/AddTargetColumnDialog.png[]

The Add Target Column button appears in two places: on the right side of the screen and below the target column names. This button will create a new target column that a transform can be connected to. Enable the 'Map this to "(no source column)"' option if the new target column does not need a source column. This will automatically create a constant transform for the new target column.

--

Once a transform has been configured, it is possible to remove it by clicking the X icon on the left or to edit it by clicking the pencil and paper icon on the right.

=== Additional Configuration Using the Canvas

image::images/canvas/ConfigCanvas.png[]

After the Transform Data screen, the initial setup stage is complete. It is also possible to exit the initial setup stage at any time by clicking the X icon in the top right corner of the sidebar.

Some additional options are available within the Configure Canvas after the initial setup stage. Clicking an endpoint or link will select it. Select an endpoint to view and edit its display name and connection details. Select a link to view and edit tables, subset rules, and transforms. When a link is selected, the Next and Previous buttons can be used to navigate between these three configuration items. In order to edit configuration across a link, the endpoints on either side of the link must have valid connection details.

Clicking the Delete button in the top right corner of the Configure Canvas will remove the selected endpoint or link from the Canvas. To deselect an endpoint or link, click the empty background of the Canvas or close the sidebar. 

To add more endpoints, click the Add Replication button. This will create two new endpoints and go through the process of configuring replication between them. It's also possible to use this button to create just one new endpoint which replicates with an existing endpoint, or itself.

Any endpoint can be linked to any other endpoint by clicking one of the four diamonds along the edge of the endpoint and dragging a line to another endpoint (or the same endpoint). To move any endpoint across the Canvas, click and drag the endpoint to the desired location.

At any time, it is possible to reset the Configure Canvas to its original state by clicking the Reset button in the bottom left corner of the Canvas. If the Canvas is reset before any configuration has been deployed, this will display the welcome screen. Later on, after deployment, resetting the Canvas will load the existing configuration from the database into the Canvas.

=== Deployment

Once the configuration is complete, it must be deployed to take effect. Click the Deploy button to open the Deploy Wizard.

==== Endpoint Overview

image::images/wizard-deploy/EndpointScreen.png[]

The first screen of the Deploy Wizard contains an overview of every undeployed endpoint. Each endpoint has several settings to configure as well as a permissions check to pass. As soon as the Deploy Wizard opens, it will start checking whether each endpoint's user has the permissions that SymmetricDS requires.

The external ID setting should be set to a name that will be used to refer to the individual endpoint. By default, it is the same as the group ID, which refers to the group that will contain this endpoint.

The capture type setting determines how SymmetricDS will capture changes to the endpoint. There are four options for capture type, though some endpoint types do not support all four:

* <<Trigger-based>> - Triggers installed and maintained by SymmetricDS to capture changes.
* <<Log-based>> - Endpoint providers transaction logs are read to capture changes.
* <<Time-based,Time-based (read-only)>> - A timestamp column or a single column numeric value is used to capture changes.
* <<Write-Only,None (write-only)>> - A node that will only act as a target and will not capture changes.

[.float-group]
--
[.left.text-left]
image::images/wizard-deploy/PermissionsDialog.png[]

Both the application database and the runtime database will go through a permissions check. When each endpoint completes its permissions check, the results will show up as either passed or failed. Click the icon to the right of each result to view more details about how each permissions check went. For more information about the specific permissions that each endpoint type requires, find the endpoint type under the <<Database Notes>> appendix.

--

The Deploy Wizard will run a performance test on any local trigger-based endpoints that pass their permissions check. After 5 seconds, the test will be complete and the performance column will contain either a green or a red icon. A green icon indicates that the endpoint has a good enough connection to be deployed locally, while a red icon indicates that it's recommended to deploy the endpoint remotely. Click the icon to view the test results, including an overall score out of 10.

The location setting can be set to either local or remote. Select local to deploy the endpoint within the current installation of SymmetricDS, or select remote if the endpoint will be deployed elsewhere. The Deploy Wizard will create all of the necessary configuration for a remote endpoint, but the endpoint itself must be deployed at a later time using the Connect Database Wizard within a separate installation of SymmetricDS. At least one endpoint must be local in order to proceed with deployment.

The sync URL setting controls the web address where the endpoint can be reached by other endpoints. Typically, it is only changed for networking and load balancing purposes. If the network will be accessing the web console through a proxy or a different hostname than the one shown, the hostname can be overridden. Also, the sync URL determines whether HTTP or HTTPS is used to transfer data. The sync URL can be accessed on the <<Nodes,Manage Nodes>> screen later.

If the capture type is not set to trigger-based, then it's possible to have SymmetricDS install its tables in a separate runtime database. The default runtime database is H2, a lightweight embedded single-user database. There are a variety of other database types that can be used for the runtime database.

The registration server option is an advanced option that determines the root-level registration server. This endpoint does not register with any other endpoint. It stores the set of configuration that will be synchronized to all other endpoints.

The "registers with" option is another advanced option that determines which endpoint the endpoint will register with. When the endpoint starts up, it will connect to this endpoint in order to join the network and receive its configuration.

==== Advanced Endpoint Settings

Sometimes, there are advanced settings that must be configured before deployment. These settings differ depending on each endpoint's type and capture type. This configuration can be done via the Advanced Settings screen.

==== Overwriting an Existing Installation

Before deployment, the Deploy Wizard will check each runtime database for an existing installation of SymmetricDS. If there is an existing installation, a warning will be displayed. Before each endpoint is deployed, the Deploy Wizard will uninstall the existing installation, if present. This means that any existing configuration will be overwritten by the new configuration that was created in the Configure Canvas.

==== Configuring Time-Based Replication

image::images/wizard-deploy/TimeBasedScreen.png[]

If any of the endpoints are using a time-based capture type, then it will be necessary to choose which columns should be checked during capture. The Deploy Wizard will check each configured table for timestamp columns or a numeric column that represents the single primary key column on the table. The field at the top of the screen can be used to enter specific column names that the Deploy Wizard should search for.

If the Deploy Wizard finds a suitable column for a table, its name will be displayed in green next to the table name. If there is more than one suitable column for a table and none of their names match the names at the top of the screen, the Deploy Wizard will allow the column name to be chosen via a dropdown field next to the table name. If no suitable columns are found for a table, then time-based change capture will not be possible for that table.

==== Configuration Change Overview

image::images/wizard-deploy/ConfigChangeScreen.png[]

After the endpoint and time-based capture screens, the Deploy Wizard will display a summary of the configuration changes from the Configure Canvas. This summary includes tables, subset rules, and transforms. Any added configuration is shown in green and any removed configuration is shown in red.

==== License Key Installation

SymmetricDS Pro requires a valid license key to be installed before deployment. For more information, see <<License Key>>.

Customers can obtain a license key from the Jumpmind accounting team. When evaluating SymmetricDS Pro via a https://www.jumpmind.com/symmetricds/trial[Proof of Concept], an expert will be available to assist with the setup process.

WARNING: Never install a trial license key in a production environment.

==== Web Console Admin User Setup

By default, SymmetricDS uses an internal security scheme for protecting the SymmetricDS application. The final step of first time setup is to set up an admin user for the SymmetricDS Pro web console. The minimum password complexity can be adjusted using the slider above the user ID field.

Additional users can be set up in the web console after the first deployment is complete. For more information, see <<Users>>.

SymmetricDS can also use LDAP, SAML, or OAuth 2.0/OpenID to secure the SymmetricDS application. For more information, see <<Single Sign-On>>.

After setting up the admin user for the web console, click Finish to proceed with deployment.

==== Deployment Details

During deployment, the Deploy Wizard will create a properties file for each endpoint. The external ID, engine name, and node group ID in each properties file will be set according to the settings from the <<Endpoint Overview>> screen. For remote endpoints, the properties file will end with `.remote`, which means that SymmetricDS will not start up local engines for these endpoints. For more information, see <<Node Properties File>>.

The Deploy Wizard will install SymmetricDS's tables into each local runtime database and create the configuration for the endpoints, links, tables, subset rules, and transforms that were defined via the Configure Canvas. Endpoint display names become <<Groups>> and links become <<Group Links>>. Tables are backed by <<Table Triggers>>, <<Table Routing>>, and <<Routers>>. Subset rules result in special <<Router Types>> and transforms are split into <<Transforms>> and <<Columns,Transform Columns>>.
